When demos appear visually unrelated (different dimensions, background colors, seed motifs, and output colors), first compare their exact input/output differences rather than assuming a fixed motif-copy rule. For every pair, record: (1) role-level colors (background, seed, painted color), (2) exact changed-cell coordinates and their bounding boxes, (3) cells left unchanged inside apparent motifs, (4) any connected components or repeated offsets involved, and (5) a concrete rule that predicts both changed and unchanged cells.

A plausible hypothesis is not validated by resemblance. Replay it across *all* demos and require exact agreement, including untouched noise and cells that retain the background. In this session, several demos had completely different grid sizes and seed shapes, and a guessed connected-zero flood-fill failed; that illustrates why a visual/connectedness guess must be checked against demo outputs before submission. After a failed answer, re-evaluate the rule itself and seek a demo that distinguishes explicit alternatives, rather than making another unsupported guess. Keep each demo request tied to a named uncertainty, since requests and wrong submissions both incur cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md"}

